"use strict";(self.webpackChunkirisxyang=self.webpackChunkirisxyang||[]).push([[372,530],{372:(e,t,i)=>{i.r(t),i.d(t,{default:()=>n});i(530);var a=i(579);function n(){return(0,a.jsxs)("div",{style:{display:"flex",flexDirection:"column",justifyContent:"center"},children:[(0,a.jsx)("h2",{className:"subheading",children:"The Ordinary World"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"My cycling journey starts a little over four years ago: Chick-Fil-A had opened its first location in Boston in January 2022. My friends and I, fiending for some fried chicken, decided to brave the Boston winter and BlueBike to the new location. This was my first time on a bike in nearly a decade, and it was true what they said--it came back to me, just like riding a bike. Unfortunately, I was awful at it."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["I distinctly remember struggling to pedal up Harvard Bridge (which, for the record, has basically no elevation)."," ",(0,a.jsxs)("span",{className:"line",children:["Years of swimming with little impact training on my feet meant I wasn't used to exerting power through my legs at all.",(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"line__popup",children:"Not to mention that everyone else in the group was on the crew team--a sport that is famously known for growing powerful, beefy legs. My twig legs and I never stood a chance."})]})," ","Also, no one tells you anything about bikes. It's kind of just assumed knowledge. I didn't know anything about shifting gears, and ended up spinning my wheels on the easiest gear and going nowhere. Not my finest moment, but we all start somewhere."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h2",{className:"subheading",children:"The Call to Adventure"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["During the summer of 2022, I decided to subscribe to an annual BlueBikes membership (discounted at $40 for MIT students)."," ",(0,a.jsxs)("span",{className:"line",children:["I needed a way to get to and from 6AM morning practices offered by Harvard University's Swimmming and Diving Program at Blodgett Pool, which was about two miles from MIT."," ",(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"line__popup",children:"While the bikes themselves proved to be fairly reliable, there was about a 25% chance that BlueBike station right outside my residence would be empty at 5:40AM. I would play my odds on this when I was feeling particularly sleepy in the morning, and let the BlueBike gods decide whether or not I made it to practice. If there were no bikes, surely it was fate telling me that swim practice was just not in the cards for the day."})]}),"The following summer, I went on my first ride ever. This was a 16-mile round trip to Ivar's Seafood Bar at the Renton waterfront. Again, I was the least experienced of the group: I was biking with a rower and another friend who had already been road biking for years. I watched in despair, dripping sweat as they would casually zoom ahead of me, slow down and wait for me to catch up, then do it all over again."]}),(0,a.jsx)("h2",{className:"subheading",children:"The Crossing of the First Threshold"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"It wasn't until my junior year in 2023 that I bought my first bike. It was a HEFTY mountain bike, affectionately dubbed \"Bike-ael\", with beefy tires and crazy looking shocks that I truly did not need in the paved streets of Cambridge. I did appreciate the grip and stability Bike-ael offered when biking through thick layers of slush and snow. It was with Bike-ael that I first rode on Boston's Minuteman Trail in the spring of 2024. A flat, somewhat poorly paved trail with roots breaking through the asphalt, which made me immensely grateful for the shocks on my beloved mountain bike. By this point, I had been commuting on bike fairly regularly and racked up hours and hours on the stairmaster. My legs were getting stronger--I was ready for another challenge."}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:'Unfortunately Bike-ael met his end that following summer, on the way to another Harvard morning practice. I ended up purchasing a less intense mountain bike dubbed "Bike-ael 2.0". I biked the Boston Marathon route for the first time with Bike-ael 2.0 in 2025--55 miles with an average pace of 12 miles per hour. Honestly, I surprised myself on this ride. At this point, I had basically spent no time on the stationary bike--only lifting and 30 minute stairmaster sessions. I was proud of my apparent progress despite the lack of explicit bike training, and the ride left me wanting more.'}),(0,a.jsx)("h2",{className:"subheading",children:"The Belly of the Whale"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"In the summer of 2025, I purchased my first ever road bike! A beautiful sleek matte black Trek Domane AL 2 Gen 4, a model recommended to me by a friend, christened Johann Sebastian Bike. This was my first time riding a bike with clipless pedals (and I absolutely ate it the first time I tried to clip out in the parking lot). I embarked on my virgin voyage from Marymoor Park, venturing up Sammamish River Trail and back. See below for a picture of me with JSB!"}),(0,a.jsx)("img",{src:"/blog_images/2026-07-jsb.jpg"}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["Throughout the summer of 2025, I was enjoying my time with JSB. I was still extremely hesitant to bike on the road--",(0,a.jsxs)("span",{className:"line",children:["I was nervous about clipping in and out at traffic lights, navigating by speeding cars, and mostly just distrustful of the drivers in Seattle."," ",(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"line__popup",children:"People will say the drivers in Boston are awful, but they're just aggressive--which is very different from being just plain bad at driving. People in Seattle literally do NOT know how to drive, which is FAR more dangerous. I cannot even begin to tell you how many times other drivers have tried to merge into me simply because they didn't see me. The bottom line is, I would not trust them to check for a bike before making a turn."})]}),"I was spending most of my time on paved trails (no car access), and the times I did venture onto the road I was always with someone else."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Nevertheless, I continued to bike more throughout my last year in Boston (2025-2026), during my Master's program at MIT. I did the Minuteman trail many more times (usually up to Nouve Bakery, my favorite bakery in Lexington), and biked the Boston Marathon route once again--this time with an average speed of 13.8 miles per hour!"}),(0,a.jsx)("h2",{className:"subheading",children:"The Road of Trials"}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"This summer (2026), I have been taking full advantage of the beautiful bike routes around Seattle. I did the full Lake Washington loop for the first time (55 miles), the full Lake Sammamish loop for the first time (21 miles), and then a double whammy Lake Washington AND Lake Sammamish combo wombo (65 miles). And rather last minute on a whim, I signed up for the annual Seattle to Portland bike ride, a 200 mile ride across two days--the most mileage I will ever have done by far."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["As I get deeper into the world of cycling, I have been doing more research on the mechanics and conventions and techniques in road biking. There is SO much to learn!"," ",(0,a.jsxs)("span",{className:"line",children:["I am just realizing how much I don't know about the sport--not to mention how apparently inaccessible the information seems."," ",(0,a.jsx)("span",{className:"line__popup",children:"The deeper I dive, the more I get the sense that the biking community (particularly the road biking community) is rather... unwelcoming. Obviously, road cycling is an incredibly inaccessible sport--a good bike will cost you over a grand at least. But it is rather disappointing that the vibe always seems to skew on the side of elitist. More on this in a later blog post."})]})," ",'For the record, when I first started on JSB I was told that I "didn\'t really have to worry about my front brake" and that I should "pretty much always be using my back brake". I also never touched my front gear (always kept it in the smaller gear), and just last week discovered that my bike has a feature called "trim clicking" or "microshifting", which is apparently an essential mechanism for shfiting on the front gear. Good thing I left it alone!']}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"Apart from the mechanical side, there is also a matter of technical skill. I rode with no hands for the first time a few weeks ago, and I have been getting better at making tight turns at higher speeds. I have also been getting more comfortable with understanding the limits of my bike and gaining confidence in my control over the bike--specifically because I got scraped up pretty bad a few weeks ago trying to avoid a Lime bike (booooo) on a road. Luckily, it was just a few surface wounds, but I am committed to avoiding another incident."}),(0,a.jsx)("h2",{className:"subheading",children:"To Be Continued..."}),(0,a.jsxs)("p",{children:["I'm really glad I took the time to reflect on these past few years and how my journey on the bike has developed over time. I'm having so much fun exploring this new world of possibilities. While it's been great to see all the progress, if I'm going to be honest, I am dissatisfied with where I am now. I ",(0,a.jsx)("strong",{children:"know"})," that I can be better."]}),(0,a.jsx)("p",{children:"The Seattle to Portland ride this upcoming weekend will mark a new milestone in my cycling career. I'll update next week--Wish me luck!"})]})}},530:(e,t,i)=>{i.r(t),i.d(t,{default:()=>a});const a={}}}]);
